## Short answer  
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on the fly** from a `raster-dem` source with the **`maplibre-contour`** library.  
The library runs a Web-Worker that reads the DEM tiles, extracts elevation isolines, and serves them to MapLibre as a virtual `vector` source. You then add a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer that points at that source.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide that works with the current MapLibre GL JS (≥ v5.6) and the latest `maplibre-contour` (v0.2-ish).  

---

## 1. Prerequisites  

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS** (v5.6 or newer) | `color-relief` and `hillshade` are only supported from v5.5, but `maplibre-contour` works with any recent version. |
| **A `raster-dem` source** that serves elevation tiles (e.g. Terrarium, Mapbox, or MapTiler DEM) | The worker reads the raw DEM values; it does **not** work with a normal raster image. |
| **`maplibre-contour`** npm package (or a bundled copy) | Provides `DemSource` and the protocol that turns DEM tiles into vector contour tiles. |
| **A modern browser** with Web-Worker support (all current browsers). | The contour extraction runs in a background thread. |

If you are using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.) you can install the library with:

```bash
npm i maplibre-contour
# or
yarn add maplibre-contour
```

If you prefer a plain-HTML page, you can pull the UMD build from a CDN:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.umd.js"></script>
```

The global variable will be `maplibreContour`.

---

## 2. Add the DEM source to the map  

First create a **`raster-dem`** source exactly the way you would for terrain or hillshade.  
Make sure the `encoding` matches the tiles you are serving (`terrarium`, `mapbox`, `raw`, …).

```javascript
// Example: Terrarium-encoded DEM tiles hosted on a tile server
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', // or .webp, .tif, …
  tileSize: 256,
  // The encoding tells the worker how to decode the RGB values.
  // Common values: 'terrarium', 'mapbox', 'raw' (float32 in a single band)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12               // match the maxzoom of your DEM tiles
});
```

> **Tip:** If you already have `map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem'})` you can reuse the same source for contours – no extra network traffic.

---

## 3. Create a `DemSource` (the contour generator)  

```javascript
import { DemSource } from 'maplibre-contour';   // or maplibreContour.DemSource for the UMD build

// 1️⃣  Build the DemSource – it knows where the DEM tiles live.
const demSource = new DemSource({
  // Same URL pattern you used for the raster-dem source.
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the encoding you gave the raster-dem source.
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom level you want the worker to be able to read.
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Set to true if you want the heavy lifting in a Web-Worker (recommended).
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣  Register the worker with the MapLibre instance.
demSource.setupMaplibre(map);
```

`setupMaplibre` does two things:

1. It registers a **custom protocol** (`mlcontour://`) that the map can request as a vector source.
2. It wires the worker so that the map can ask for contour tiles on-demand.

---

## 4. Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the contour protocol  

The protocol URL is generated by `demSource.contourProtocolUrl(options)`.  
You can control:

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `thresholds` | An object mapping **zoom → `[minor, major]`** contour intervals (in metres). The worker will emit two levels of lines (minor & major). |
| `elevationKey` | Property name that will hold the raw elevation value on each feature (default: `'ele'`). |
| `levelKey` | Property name that indicates whether a line is *minor* (`0`) or *major* (`1`). |
| `contourLayer` | Name of the internal vector layer (you’ll reference this in `source-layer`). |

```javascript
// Choose contour spacing that makes sense for each zoom level.
const thresholds = {
  // zoom : [minor interval, major interval]
  10: [200, 1000],
  11: [100, 500],
  12: [50, 200],
  13: [25, 100],
  14: [10, 50],
  15: [5, 20]
};

map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The URL is a *protocol* that the worker intercepts.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      thresholds,
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // optional – defaults to 'ele'
      levelKey: 'level',     // optional – defaults to 'level'
      contourLayer: 'contours' // internal layer name
    })
  ],
  // The vector source must advertise a maxzoom that is at least as high
  // as the highest zoom you request contours for.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

> **Why a vector source?**  
> The worker returns **MVT** (Mapbox Vector Tile) data that MapLibre can render like any other vector source. This means you can style it with the full power of expressions, data-driven styling, and even label it with `symbol` layers.

---

## 5. Add a line layer (and optionally a label layer)  

```javascript
// 1️⃣  The actual contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` we gave above
  paint: {
    // Use the `level` property to differentiate major/minor lines.
    // Here we give major lines a darker colour and a bit thicker.
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, // major
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)',
      // minor
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)'
    ],
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, // major
      1.5,
      0.8 // minor
    ],
    // Optional: make the lines fade out at low zooms
    'line-opacity': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['zoom'],
      10, 0.6,
      14, 1
    ]
  }
});

// 2️⃣  Optional: label the major contours with their elevation value
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']], // elevation in metres
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.9)',
    'text-halo-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.8)',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

That’s it – the map will now request DEM tiles, the worker will compute isolines for the current viewport/zoom, and the vector layer will draw them.

---

## 6. Performance & tuning tips  

| Issue | How to mitigate |
|-------|-----------------|
| **CPU spikes when zooming fast** | Increase `maxzoom` of the DEM source only as high as you need. The worker only processes tiles up to that zoom. |
| **Too many contour lines at high zoom** | Use finer `thresholds` (smaller intervals) only at higher zooms, and coarser intervals at low zooms (as shown in the `thresholds` object). |
| **Memory pressure on low-end devices** | Set `worker: false` to run the algorithm on the main thread (slower but uses less memory) **or** limit the number of concurrent workers via `demSource.maxWorkers = 2`. |
| **Incorrect elevation values** | Verify that the `encoding` you pass to `DemSource` matches the DEM tiles. `terrarium` expects `R*256 + G + B/256 - 32768`. `mapbox` expects `R*256*256 + G*256 + B`. |
| **Missing contours on some tiles** | Ensure the DEM source’s `maxzoom` is **≥** the highest zoom you request contours for, and that the DEM tiles actually exist at those zoom levels. |
| **Styling lag** | Cache the generated vector tiles by adding a `cache-control` header on the server, or use a Service Worker to store them locally. |

---

