Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the blank page-background you see above the horizon into a real sky with haze (fog) that follows the terrain.  
All of the code works with **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6** (the version that introduced the `sky` object).

---

## 1. What’s missing?

* The map already has `map.setTerrain(...)` and a pitch of 70°, so the 3-D mesh is drawn correctly.  
* Anything that is **above the terrain mesh** is drawn from the *background* layer (the default solid colour of the page).  
* MapLibre does **not** have a `setFog()` method and it does **not** have a `sky` *layer* type.  
* The sky (and its fog/haze) is a **root-level style object** that you add with `map.setSky()` (or by putting a `sky` object directly into the style JSON).

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- DEM source (raster-dem) ---------------------------------
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // ← your DEM TileJSON
          tileSize: 256,
          // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (default is mapbox)
        },
        // ---- Some imagery source (optional) -------------------------
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // ---- Background – make it transparent so the sky shows ----------
        { id: 'bg', type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' } },

        // ---- Imagery draped over the terrain -----------------------------
        {
          id: 'satellite',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'sat'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // any lon/lat you like
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // <-- you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // important for smooth terrain shading
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  When the style is ready, turn on terrain
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // 2a – enable the DEM as terrain
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      exaggeration: 1.0   // increase only if you really need it
    });

    // 2b – add the sky (this also creates the fog/haze)
    map.setSky({
      // ---- Sky colour (the “day-sky” colour) -------------------------
      'sky-color': '#80a0ff',          // light blue
      // ---- How the sky blends into the horizon ----------------------
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // 0 = hard line, 1 = very soft
      // ---- Colour of the horizon line itself ------------------------
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',     // white-ish horizon
      // ---- Fog (haze) that appears where the ground meets the sky ----
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // same hue as sky, a bit lighter
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,        // how quickly fog fades into the horizon
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,         // how quickly fog fades into the terrain
      // ---- Optional extra blend for a more atmospheric look ----------
      'atmosphere-blend': 0.5
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  (Optional) tweak the sky / fog to taste
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // You can call setSky again later to animate the sky or change the
    // colours based on UI controls.
    // Example: make the sky darker at night
    // map.setSky({ 'sky-color': '#0b1d3a', 'fog-color': '#001020' });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | Code | Why it matters |
|------|------|----------------|
| **Background layer** | `type: "background", paint: {"background-color":"rgba(0,0,0,0)"}` | Makes the *page background* transparent so the sky you add later can be seen. If you leave the default opaque background you’ll still see a solid colour above the horizon. |
| **Terrain** | `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem',exaggeration:1})` | Turns the DEM into a 3-D mesh. The sky/fog only works when terrain is active. |
| **Sky** | `map.setSky({ … })` | Adds a root-level sky object. The same object also defines the fog/haze. |
| **Fog-related properties** | `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend` | These are the only way to get haze in MapLibre. They are *not* a separate `setFog` call. |
| **Pitch** | `pitch: 70` (or any > 0) | Without pitch the terrain is seen from straight above and the sky never becomes visible. |

---

## 3. Understanding the sky-fog parameters

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| **`sky-color`** | any CSS colour | Base colour of the sky dome. |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | `0 … 1` | How sharply the sky colour meets the horizon line. Larger values give a smoother gradient. |
| **`horizon-color`** | any CSS colour | Colour of the thin line where sky meets terrain. Often white or a very light version of `sky-color`. |
| **`fog-color`** | any CSS colour (usually similar to `sky-color`) | Colour of the haze that appears near the horizon. |
| **`horizon-fog-blend`** | `0 … 1` | Controls how quickly the fog fades *into* the horizon line. Smaller = a crisp horizon, larger = a more “misty” look. |
| **`fog-ground-blend`** | `0 … 1` | Controls how quickly the fog fades *into* the terrain. Larger values make the fog extend farther down the slopes. |
| **`atmosphere-blend`** (optional) | `0 … 1` | Adds a subtle atmospheric scattering effect; useful for a more realistic look. |

Play with these values until the sky looks the way you want. A good starting point for a bright day is the set shown in the example; for a sunset you could use a deeper orange for `sky-color` and a pinkish `fog-color`.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Above-horizon area stays solid white/grey** | The background layer is opaque (default) or you never called `setSky`. | Add a transparent background layer (as in the example) **or** set `background-color` to the same colour you use for `sky-color`. |
| **Fog does nothing** | Terrain is not enabled, or you are using a MapLibre version < 5.6. | Ensure `map.setTerrain(...)` is called **and** you are on v5.6+ (check `map.version`). |
| **`map.setSky is not a function`** | Using an old MapLibre version (< 5.5). | Upgrade to the latest release (`npm i maplibre-gl@latest` or use the CDN link above). |
| **Horizon line is a hard black edge** | `sky-horizon-blend` is `0` (default) and `horizon-color` is black. | Set `sky-horizon-blend` > 0 and give `horizon-color` a light colour. |
| **Fog looks too thick / disappears too fast** | `horizon-fog-blend` or `fog-ground-blend` are set to extremes. | Adjust those two values gradually (0.2-0.6 is a good range). |
| **The sky disappears when you change the style** | You added the sky via `setSky` *before* the style finished loading. | Call `setSky` inside `map.on('style.load')` or `map.on('load')` (as shown). |

---

## 5. Optional: Adding a “night-time” sky toggle

If you want to let the user switch between day and night, you can simply call `setSky` again with a different colour palette:

```js
function setDaySky() {
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#80a0ff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.5
  });
}

function setNightSky() {
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#0b1d3a',          // deep navy
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.6,
    'horizon-color': '#2c3e50',
    'fog-color': '#001020',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.6,
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.7
  });
}

// Example UI
document.getElementById('toggle').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const isDay = map.getSky().skyColor === '#80a0ff';
  isDay ? setNightSky() : setDaySky();
});
```

The `map.getSky()` method (available from v5.9) lets you read the current sky settings if you need to know the current mode.

---

## 6. TL;DR – The minimal code you need

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem' });

  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#80a0ff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.5
  });
});
```

Add a **transparent background layer** (or set its colour to match the sky) and you’ll see a proper sky with haze instead of the blank page background.

---

### 🎉 You’re done!

Your map now has:

* **3-D terrain** (already working)
* **A sky dome** that fills the space above the horizon
* **Fog/haze** that blends the terrain into the sky, giving a realistic atmospheric effect

Feel free to tweak the colour and blend values until the look matches your design. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a quick-to-copy recipe for **adding a pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG basemap** to a MapLibre GL JS map and keeping all of your vector data (including label layers) on top.

> **Key point** – A JPEG raster tileset is *just a raster source*.  
> It is **not** a `raster-dem` source, so none of the hillshade / terrain / sky APIs apply.  
> Treat it like any other raster basemap: add a `raster` source, add a `raster` layer, and make sure that layer sits **below** every vector layer you want to draw on top.

---

## 1. Add the raster source

```js
// 1️⃣  Define the source that points at your JPEG tiles.
//     Adjust the URL template, tileSize (usually 256), and maxzoom to match your tileset.
map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    // Example: https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg
    'https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,          // most raster tiles are 256 px; change if yours are 512
  maxzoom: 14,            // set to the highest zoom you have
  attribution: '© YourProvider'   // optional but recommended
});
```

*If you are building a static style JSON instead of adding the source at runtime, the same object goes under the `"sources"` block.*

---

## 2. Add the raster layer

```js
// 2️⃣  Add a raster layer that draws the source.
//     Place it at the very bottom of the layer stack (or before the first vector layer you want on top).
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'relief-basemap',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'relief-basemap',
    // Optional paint properties:
    paint: {
      // Make the basemap a little transparent if you want to see a background colour underneath.
      // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
      // Control how quickly tiles fade in when they load.
      // 'raster-fade-duration': 300
    },
    // You can limit the zoom range if you only want the basemap at certain levels.
    minzoom: 0,
    maxzoom: 14
  },
  // 👇  Insert the layer *before* the first label layer (or any other layer you want on top).
  // If you don’t know the id of the first label layer, you can use `null` to append at the end,
  // then later move the label layers above it with `moveLayer`.
  // Example: map.addLayer(..., 'water'); // puts the basemap right before the 'water' layer.
  // For a generic “put it at the bottom” you can pass the id of the first layer in the style:
  map.getStyle().layers[0].id   // <-- this works only after the style has loaded.
);
```

### Why the `before` argument matters

MapLibre draws layers **in the order they appear in the style**.  
If you add the raster layer *after* your vector layers, it will cover them.  
Pass the id of the first vector layer (often something like `"water"` or `"landuse"`), or simply add the raster layer first and then add all your vector layers later.

If you already have a full style loaded and you’re not sure where to insert the raster layer, you can always reposition it later:

```js
// Move the raster layer to the bottom of the stack:
map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', map.getStyle().layers[0].id);
```

---

## 3. Keep your label (or any other vector) layers on top

If you already have a label layer called `"place-labels"` (or any other vector layer), make sure it is added **after** the raster layer, or explicitly move it above:

```js
// Example: ensure the label layer is above the basemap
map.moveLayer('place-labels', 'relief-basemap'); // puts it right after the basemap
```

You can repeat `moveLayer` for every vector layer you want to guarantee stays on top (e.g., roads, points, symbols).

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief JPEG basemap + vector labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // 1️⃣  Add the raster source here (optional – you can also add it later with addSource)
        'relief-basemap': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14,
          attribution: '© YourProvider'
        },
        // Example vector source (e.g., OpenStreetMap data)
        'osm': {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf' // replace with your own
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // 2️⃣  Raster basemap layer – placed first so everything else draws on top
        {
          id: 'relief-basemap',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'relief-basemap',
          paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1 }
        },
        // 3️⃣  Example vector layers (roads, labels, etc.)
        {
          id: 'road-lines',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'transportation',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#fff', 'line-width': 2 }
        },
        {
          id: 'place-labels',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'place',
          layout: {
            'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
            'text-size': 12,
            'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
          },
          paint: {
            'text-color': '#000',
            'text-halo-color': '#fff',
            'text-halo-width': 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the `tiles` URL with the actual endpoint that serves your JPEG tiles, and swap the vector source for whatever you’re using (e.g., Mapbox Vector Tiles, GeoJSON, etc.).*

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Your JPEG tiles cover the labels** | Raster layer added *after* the label layer (or `moveLayer` placed it later). | Add the raster layer first, or call `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'place-labels')` to push it below. |
| **Tiles look blurry / pixelated** | Wrong `tileSize` (e.g., using 256 when the source is 512) or missing `tileSize` altogether. | Set `tileSize` to the size your tiles are generated at (256 px is default). |
| **Only part of the map shows the basemap** | `maxzoom`/`minzoom` on the layer or source is too restrictive. | Ensure `maxzoom` matches the highest zoom you have; remove `minzoom`/`maxzoom` if you want it visible everywhere. |
| **CORS errors** | Your tile server does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers. | Enable CORS on the tile server, or proxy the tiles through a server you control. |
| **You want the basemap to fade in smoothly** | Default `raster-fade-duration` is 300 ms, but you see a hard pop. | Set `'raster-fade-duration': 0` in the layer’s `paint` to disable fading, or increase the value for a slower fade. |
| **You later decide you need 3-D terrain** | JPEG tiles are pre-rendered; they cannot be extruded. | You’ll need a true `raster-dem` source (e.g., Terrarium, Mapbox, or custom DEM tiles) and a `hillshade`/`color-relief`/`setTerrain` pipeline. The JPEG basemap can still be used as a *fallback* background, but it won’t give you 3-D relief. |

---

## 6. When you *do* want real terrain (just for reference)

If you ever need a true 3-D terrain surface (instead of a static shaded-relief image), you’ll have to:

1. Obtain a DEM (e.g., Terrarium, Mapbox, or custom `raster-dem` tiles).  
2. Add it as a `raster-dem` source.  
3. Call `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5});`  
4. (Optional) Add a `hillshade` or `color-relief` layer for visual shading.  

That is a completely different workflow from the JPEG basemap you’re using now, and the two cannot be mixed for the same terrain effect.

---

### TL;DR

```js
// 1️⃣ source
map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});

// 2️⃣ layer (add it before any vector layers)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'relief-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'relief-basemap',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1 }
}, firstVectorLayerId);   // <-- replace with the id of the layer you want it before

// 3️⃣ ensure labels stay on top
map.moveLayer('place-labels', 'relief-basemap'); // or just add them after the raster layer
```

That’s all you need to get a beautiful pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG basemap under your vector data, with labels (or any other vector layers) staying crisp on top. Happy mapping!

### TL;DR  
`map.setFog()` is a **Mapbox-GL-JS-only** method.  
In MapLibre GL JS you get the same “haze-at-the-horizon” effect by adding a **sky object** (or calling `map.setSky()`) and configuring its fog-related properties.  
The fog only appears when 3-D terrain is active (or the map is pitched) – otherwise the sky is just a solid background colour.

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste ready snippet that reproduces the classic Mapbox `setFog` call with MapLibre-compatible code.

---

## 1. What changed?

| Mapbox GL JS (v2) | MapLibre GL JS (v2.x / v3.x) |
|-------------------|------------------------------|
| `map.setFog({ "color": "...", "highColor": "...", "horizonBlend": 0.5, "range": [0.8, 8] })` | **No `setFog` method**. Fog is part of the **sky** object. |
| Fog works even without terrain (it just blends into the background). | Fog is only rendered when **terrain is enabled** (or the map is pitched) because it is tied to the atmospheric scattering model of the sky. |
| Fog properties are `color`, `highColor`, `horizonBlend`, `range`. | Fog properties are `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend` (plus the usual sky colours). |

So the migration steps are:

1. **Add a sky object** (via style JSON or `map.setSky()`).
2. **Configure fog** inside that sky object.
3. **Enable terrain** (or at least pitch the map) so the fog can be drawn.

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Horizon haze (fog) replacement for setFog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style that has a raster-dem source works)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // demo style with a DEM source
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,               // pitch is required for fog to be visible
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true          // smoother terrain rendering
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (if your style does not already contain one)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // If the style already has a raster-dem source you can skip this.
    // Example source name: "terrain-dem"
    if (!map.getSource('terrain-dem')) {
      map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // replace with your DEM tiles
        tileSize: 256,
        maxzoom: 12
      });
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Enable 3-D terrain (required for fog)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add a sky object with fog (this replaces map.setFog)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // ---- sky colours (optional, but look nice) ----
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',          // deep-blue at zenith
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // how quickly the sky colour blends to the horizon colour
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',     // near-white at the horizon

      // ---- fog (the haze) ----
      // fog-color is the colour of the haze that blends with the terrain.
      // It is interpreted as an sRGB colour; you can use any CSS colour string.
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',         // light blue haze (similar to Mapbox default)
      // horizon-fog-blend controls how much fog is visible near the horizon.
      // 0 = no fog at horizon, 1 = full fog at horizon.
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      // fog-ground-blend controls how quickly the fog fades as you look down
      // toward the ground. 0 = no fog near the ground, 1 = fog reaches the ground.
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Add a hillshade or color-relief layer so you can see the effect
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Example hillshade layer – you can omit if you already have one.
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)',
        // Use the newer, softer method (no need to stack layers)
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Any MapLibre style works; the demo style already contains a raster-dem source called `terrain`. |
| **2️⃣ Add a DEM source** | If your style does not already have a `raster-dem` source, add one. The source name (`terrain-dem` in the example) is what you’ll reference later. |
| **3️⃣ Enable terrain** | `map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem' })` creates the 3-D mesh that the sky-fog algorithm needs. |
| **4️⃣ Set the sky (fog)** | `map.setSky()` creates a root-level `sky` object. The three `fog-*` properties are the direct replacements for the old `setFog` options. |
| **5️⃣ (Optional) Hillshade** | Adding a hillshade layer lets you see the terrain relief under the haze. The `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` gives a soft, natural look without stacking multiple layers. |

---

## 3. Mapping the old `setFog` options to the new `sky` properties

| Mapbox `setFog` option | MapLibre `sky` equivalent | Typical value (matches Mapbox default) |
|------------------------|---------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| `color` (horizon fog colour) | `fog-color` | `'#c0d8ff'` (light blue) |
| `highColor` (sky colour at zenith) | `sky-color` | `'#199EF3'` |
| `horizonBlend` (how quickly fog blends to horizon) | `horizon-fog-blend` | `0.5` |
| `range` (near/far fog distances) | *Not directly exposed* – the effect is controlled by `horizon-fog-blend` and `fog-ground-blend`. The defaults (`0.5/0.5`) give a similar visual depth. |

If you need a tighter or looser fog you can tweak the two blend values:

```js
map.setSky({
  'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.7, // more fog near the horizon
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.3   // less fog near the ground
});
```

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No haze at all, even after `setSky`** | Terrain not enabled **or** map pitch is `0`. | Call `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` **and** set `pitch` > 0 (e.g., `map.easeTo({pitch:60})`). |
| **Fog looks like a solid colour, no gradient** | `horizon-fog-blend` and `fog-ground-blend` both set to `0` (or omitted). | Use non-zero blend values (0.3-0.7 range works well). |
| **Fog colour is wrong (too dark / too bright)** | Using a CSS colour that is not sRGB or forgetting the leading `#`. | Use a proper hex or `rgba()` string. |
| **`map.setSky` throws “is not a function”** | Using a MapLibre version < 2.0 (sky was added in v2.0). | Upgrade to at least **MapLibre GL JS 2.0** (or the current 4.x). |
| **Performance drops heavily** | Terrain + sky + many draped raster layers at high zoom. | Keep the DEM source at a reasonable `maxzoom` (e.g., 12), limit hillshade opacity, and consider disabling terrain on low-end devices. |

---

## 5. Full-style JSON alternative (if you prefer to bake the sky into the style)

If you manage your map style as a JSON file (or via a style editor), you can embed the sky directly:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "terrain-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 12
    },
    "... other sources ..."
  },
  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain-dem",
    "exaggeration": 1
  },
  "sky": {
    "sky-type": "atmosphere",          // optional, default is "atmosphere"
    "sky-color": "#199EF3",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "hillshade",
      "type": "hillshade",
      "source": "terrain-dem",
      "paint": {
        "hillshade-method": "multidirectional",
        "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.4,
        "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "map",
        "hillshade-shadow-color": "rgba(30,40,80,0.45)",
        "hillshade-highlight-color": "rgba(255,240,200,0.4)"
      }
    },
    "... other layers ..."
  ]
}
```

When the style loads, the sky (with fog) is automatically applied; you do **not** need to call `map.setSky()` at runtime.

---

## 6. Quick checklist for a successful migration

1. **Upgrade** to a recent MapLibre version (≥ 2.0).  
2. **Remove** any `map.setFog(...)` calls – they will throw “is not a function”.  
3. **Add** a `raster-dem` source (if not already present).  
4. **Enable terrain** with `map.setTerrain({source:'your-dem'})`.  
5. **Add a sky** (via `map.setSky()` **or** a `sky` object in the style).  
6. **Configure fog** using `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`.  
7. **Pitch** the map (≥ 30°) to see the effect.  
8. **Test** on a low-end device; if it’s too heavy, lower the DEM `maxzoom` or disable terrain on that device.

---

### TL;DR code snippet (the absolute minimum)

```js
// after map has loaded and you have a raster-dem source called "terrain-dem"
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem' });

map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});

// make sure the map is pitched so the fog can be seen
map.easeTo({ pitch: 60 });
```

That reproduces the classic Mapbox `setFog` haze in a MapLibre-only environment. Happy mapping!
